styx sandbox — reading this image
=================================

This is the image a styx session runs `bash` and `code_exec` in. The harness's
prompt is normative for how tools are run; this file mirrors, inside the
container, the parts of that convention a command needs. Where the two
disagree the prompt wins, and the parser contract is the normative version.

Artifacts
---------
Write raw scanner output to the project's `.styx/out/` directory (bind-mounted
at /workspace). The harness watches it and parses what appears; a scanner run
whose output is hand-grepped never reaches the engagement record.

  nmap          -oX .styx/out/<name>.xml
  nuclei        -jsonl -o .styx/out/<name>.jsonl
  gobuster      -o .styx/out/<name>.txt
  sqlmap        --output-dir .styx/out
  netexec       --log .styx/out/<name>.log
  bloodhound.py --zip .styx/out/<name>.zip

ffuf, semgrep and trivy have no parser: their output is read by the model, and
findings enter engagement state through the audited state_update tool with the
artifact attached as evidence.

Wordlists
---------
STYX_WORDLISTS=/usr/share/wordlists/styx. The toolset manifest declares each
list's sha256, so a swapped or truncated list is detectable.

The toolset manifest
--------------------
/etc/styx/toolset.json declares every tool, its version, and the parser that
understands its output (null for the tools the model parses itself).
/etc/styx/build-info.json records the exact pinned inputs this image was built
from: base digest, every package and version, the tool-data versions, the
build clock, and the manifest's own hash.

Offline by design
-----------------
Tool data is baked at build, never fetched at runtime: nuclei templates,
semgrep rules, the trivy vulnerability and Java databases, and this wordlist
subset. `nuclei -update-templates` and a trivy database update therefore fail at
the session's egress boundary, by design. Updating any of them means a new
image release.
